"strong incentive", "provide an incentive", "economic incentive" ], "synonyms": ["inducement", "motivation", "stimulus", "bonus"], "near_synonyms": [ \{ "word": "motivation", "difference": "motivation refers to the internal psychological desire or drive to do something; incentive is the external reward (like money or gifts) setup to cause that drive." \}, \{ "word": "stimulus", "difference": "stimulus is an environmental or medical trigger that causes an immediate physical or automatic reaction." \} ], "antonyms": ["deterrent", "discouragement", "disincentive"], "word_family": [ \{ "word": "incentive", "pos": "noun" \}, \{ "word": "incentivize", "pos": "verb" \} ], "usage_note": "'Financial incentive' and 'tax incentive' are highly standard terms used in corporate finance and government policy discussions.", "frequency_rank": 26, "frequency_source": "custom", "difficulty": "B2", "tags": ["college", "business", "economics"] \}, \{ "word": "incorporate", "pos": "verb", "phonetic": "/\u618 n\u712 k\u596 \u720 rp\u601 re\u618 t/", "definition": "To take in or include something as a part of a larger whole, group, or system.", "definition_zh": "\u32013 \u20837 \u65307 \u20341 \u20837 \u65307 \u23559 \'85\u21512 \u20341 \u65307 \u21253 \u21547 ", "etymology": \{ "roots": [ \{ "root": "in", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "into", "meaning_zh": "\u36914 \u20837 \u12289 \u25237 \u20837 " \}, \{ "root": "corpor / corpus", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "body", "meaning_zh": "\u36523 \u39636 \u12289 \u22296 \u39636 " \} ] \}, "mnemonic": \{ "root_memory": "IN\u65288 \u36914 \u20837 \u65289 + CORPOR\u65288 \u36523 \u39636 \u65289 \u8594  \u25226 \u26032 \u20803 \u32032 \u21512 \u32232 \u25104 \u21516 \u19968 \u20491 \u36523 \u39636 \u35041 ", "story_en": "The designer decided to build IN (into) the main CORPOR (body) of the dress a set of tech fibers to INCORPORATE style and safety.", "story_zh": "\u35373 \u35336 \u24107 \u27770 \u23450 \u23559 \u31185 \u25216 \u32406 \u32173 \u12300 \u27880 \u20837 \u65288 IN\u65289 \u12301 \u26381 \u35037 \u30340 \u12300 \u20027 \u39636 \u65288 CORPOR\u65289 \u12301 \u20013 \u65292 \u20197 \u27492 \u20358 \u12300 \u32080 \u21512 \u65288 INCORPORATE\u65289 \u12301 \u26178 \u23578 \u33287 \u23433 \u20840 \u12290 ", "emojis": ["\u55357 \u56580 ", "\u55358 \u56809 ", "\u55356 \u57314 "] \}, "example": "The new version of the software incorporates several features requested by users.", "example_zh": "\u35442 \u36575 \u39636 \u30340 \u26032 \u29256 \u26412 \u32013 \u20837 \u20102 \u20351 \u29992 \u32773 \u35201 \u27714 \u30340 \u24190 \u38917 \u21151 \u33021 \u12290 ", "collocations": [ "incorporate features", "incorporate into", "seamlessly incorporate", "decide to incorporate", "incorporate ideas" ], "synonyms": ["integrate", "assimilate", "absorb", "embody"], "near_synonyms": [ \{ "word": "integrate", "difference": "integrate emphasizes combining two parts so smoothly that they blend together and work as one system; incorporate emphasizes adding something as an extra sub-component into an existing body." \}, \{ "word": "include", "difference": "include is a general term meaning to contain as part of a list, without implying structural reorganization." \} ], "antonyms": ["exclude", "separate", "segregate"], "word_family": [ \{ "word": "incorporate", "pos": "verb" \}, \{ "word": "incorporation", "pos": "noun" \}, \{ "word": "incorporated", "pos": "adjective" \} ], "usage_note": "In corporate law, 'Incorporated' (abbreviated as Inc.) means a business has legally formed a distinct legal body/entity.", "frequency_rank": 27, "frequency_source": "custom", "difficulty": "B2", "tags": ["college", "business", "academic"] \}, \{ "word": "inevitable", "pos": "adjective", "phonetic": "/\u618 n\u712 ev\u618 t\u601 bl/", "definition": "Certain to happen; unavoidable because of natural laws or logical sequence.", "definition_zh": "\u19981 \u21487 \u36991 \u20813 \u30340 \u65307 \u24517 \u28982 \u30332 \u29983 \u30340 ", "etymology": \{ "roots": [ \{ "root": "in", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "not", "meaning_zh": "\u19981 \u12289 \u38750 " \}, \{ "root": "e / ex", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "out, away", "meaning_zh": "\u21521 \u22806 \u12289 \u38626 \u38283 " \}, \{ "root": "vit / vitare", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "to avoid", "meaning_zh": "\u36530 \u36991 " \} ] \}, "mnemonic": \{ "root_memory": "IN\u65288 \u19981 \u65289 + E\u65288 \u22806 \u65289 + VIT\u65288 \u36530 \u65289 \u8594  \u19981 \u33021 \u24448 \u22806 \u36530 \u38283 \u65292 \u36991 \u28961 \u21487 \u36991 ", "story_en": "It is IN (not) possible to run E (out) and VIT (avoid) aging; it is an INEVITABLE part of life.", "story_zh": "\u20154 \u12300 \u19981 \u65288 IN\u65289 \u12301 \u21487 \u33021 \u36867 \u12300 \u21521 \u22806 \u65288 E\u65289 \u12301 \u21435 \u12300 \u36530 \u36991 \u65288 VIT\u65289 \u12301 \u32769 \u21270 \u65307 \u36889 \u26159 \u29983 \u21629 \u20013 \u12300 \u19981 \u21487 \u36991 \u20813 \u30340 \u65288 INEVITABLE\u65289 \u12301 \u19968 \u37096 \u20998 \u12290 ", "emojis": ["\u9203 ", "\u55356 \u57154 ", "\u9968 \u65039 "] \}, "example": "With the bridge heavily collapsed, a massive traffic delay across the district became inevitable.", "example_zh": "\u30001 \u26044 \u27211 \u27153 \u22196 \u37325 \u22604 \u38519 \u65292 \u35442 \u22320 \u21312 \u30332 \u29983 \u22823 \u35215 \u27169 \u20132 \u36890 \u24310 \u35492 \u35722 \u24471 \u19981 \u21487 \u36991 \u20813 \u12290 ", "collocations": [ "inevitable outcome", "seem inevitable", "virtually inevitable", "inevitable consequence", "accept the inevitable" ], "synonyms": ["unavoidable", "inescapable", "fated", "certain"], "near_synonyms": [ \{ "word": "unavoidable", "difference": "unavoidable applies to immediate physical disruptions (like an unavoidable accident on a road); inevitable often describes broad, historic, philosophical, or natural shifts over time." \}, \{ "word": "certain", "difference": "certain is a general state of absolute knowledge or confidence, whereas inevitable focuses on the unstoppable forward movement of an event." \} ], "antonyms": ["evitable", "avoidable", "preventable"], "word_family": [ \{ "word": "inevitable", "pos": "adjective" \}, \{ "word": "inevitability", "pos": "noun" \}, \{ "word": "inevitably", "pos": "adverb" \} ], "usage_note": "The noun phrase 'accept the inevitable' means to resign oneself to a coming fate or destiny that cannot be altered.", "frequency_rank": 28, "frequency_source": "custom", "difficulty": "B2", "tags": ["college", "philosophy", "academic"] \}, \{ "word": "infrastructure", "pos": "noun", "phonetic": "/\u712 \u618 nfr\u601 str\u652 kt\u643 \u601 r/", "definition": "The basic physical and organizational structures and facilities (e.g., buildings, roads, power supplies) needed for the operation of a society or enterprise.", "definition_zh": "\u22522 \u30990 \u24314 \u35373 \u65307 \u22522 \u30990 \u35373 \u26045 ", "etymology": \{ "roots": [ \{ "root": "infra", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "below, underneath", "meaning_zh": "\u22312 \'85\u20043 \u19979 \u12289 \u24213 \u23652 " \}, \{ "root": "struct / struere", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "to build", "meaning_zh": "\u24314 \u36896 \u12289 \u24314 \u31689 " \} ] \}, "mnemonic": \{ "root_memory": "INFRA\u65288 \u22312 \'85\u20043 \u19979 \u65289 + STRUCT\u65288 \u24314 \u36896 \u65289 \u8594  \u24314 \u36896 \u22312 \u22478 \u24066 \u24213 \u23652 \u25903 \u25345 \u36939 \u20316 \u30340 \u22522 \u30707 ", "story_en": "Workers must build INFRA (below) the ground to set up the water STRUCT (structure) for the city's INFRASTRUCTURE.", "story_zh": "\u24037 \u20154 \u24517 \u38920 \u22312 \u22320 \u38754 \u12300 \u20043 \u19979 \u65288 INFRA\u65289 \u12301 \u12300 \u24314 \u36896 \u65288 STRUCT\u65289 \u12301 \u20379 \u27700 \u31649 \u36947 \u65292 \u36889 \u27083 \u25104 \u20102 \u22478 \u24066 \u30340 \u12300 \u22522 \u30990 \u24314 \u35373 \u65288 INFRASTRUCTURE\u65289 \u12301 \u12290 ", "emojis": ["\u55356 \u57303 \u65039 ", "\u55357 \u57059 \u65039 ", "\u55357 \u56964 "] \}, "example": "Developing countries need substantial foreign investment to upgrade their aging transport infrastructure.", "example_zh": "\u38283 \u30332 \u20013 \u22283 \u23478 \u38656 \u35201 \u22823 \u37327 \u30340 \u22806 \u22283 \u25237 \u36039 \u20358 \u21319 \u32026 \u20854 \u32769 \u33290 \u30340 \u20132 \u36890 \u22522 \u30990 \u35373 \u26045 \u12290 ", "collocations": [ "aging infrastructure", "critical infrastructure", "transport infrastructure", "infrastructure development", "digital infrastructure" ], "synonyms": ["substructure", "foundation", "framework", "base"], "near_synonyms": [ \{ "word": "foundation", "difference": "foundation refers to the solid concrete base underneath a single specific building or an abstract idea; infrastructure is a massive network supporting an entire city or nation." \}, \{ "word": "framework", "difference": "framework refers to a logical structure, conceptual set of rules, or a physical skeleton, rather than public utility systems." \} ], "antonyms": ["superstructure"], "word_family": [ \{ "word": "infrastructure", "pos": "noun" \}, \{ "word": "infrastructural", "pos": "adjective" \} ], "usage_note": "In modern corporate language, 'digital infrastructure' refers to servers, cloud data centers, and internet networks that support business software.", "frequency_rank": 29, "frequency_source": "custom", "difficulty": "B2", "tags": ["college", "economics", "politics"] \}, \{ "word": "inherent", "pos": "adjective", "phonetic": "/\u618 n\u712 h\u618 r\u601 nt/", "definition": "Existing in something as a permanent, essential, or characteristic attribute; belonging by nature.", "definition_zh": "\u22266 \u26377 \u30340 \u65307 \u20839 \u22312 \u30340 \u65307 \u29983 \u20358 \u23601 \u26377 \u30340 ", "etymology": \{ "roots": [ \{ "root": "in", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "in, within", "meaning_zh": "\u22312 \'85\u20839 \u37096 " \}, \{ "root": "her / haerere", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "to stick", "meaning_zh": "\u40655 \u36028 \u12289 \u32202 \u36028 " \} ] \}, "mnemonic": \{ "root_memory": "IN\u65288 \u22312 \'85\u20839 \u65289 + HER\u65288 \u40655 \u20303 \u65289 \u8594  \u24478 \u20986 \u29983 \u38283 \u22987 \u23601 \u28145 \u28145 \u40655 \u22312 \u39592 \u23376 \u35041 \u30340 \u29305 \u24615 ", "story_en": "The love for art was stuck IN (within) his mind, a trait that would HER (stick) as an INHERENT passion.", "story_zh": "\u23565 \u34269 \u34899 \u30340 \u29105 \u24859 \u12300 \u40655 \u65288 HER\u65289 \u12301 \u22312 \u28145 \u34389 \u12300 \u20839 \u37096 \u65288 IN\u65289 \u12301 \u65292 \u36889 \u26159 \u20182 \u12300 \u22266 \u26377 \u30340 \u65288 INHERENT\u65289 \u12301 \u29105 \u24773 \u12290 ", "emojis": ["\u55358 \u56812 ", "\u55358 \u56800 ", "\u55357 \u56462 "] \}, "example": "Every financial investment in the stock market involves an inherent risk of losing capital.", "example_zh": "\u32929 \u31080 \u24066 \u22580 \u20013 \u30340 \u27599 \u38917 \u37329 \u34701 \u25237 \u36039 \u37117 \u21253 \u21547 \u22833 \u21435 \u36039 \u26412 \u30340 \u22266 \u26377 \u39080 \u38570 \u12290 ", "collocations": [ "inherent risk", "inherent danger", "inherent bias", "inherent worth", "inherent in" ], "synonyms": ["innate", "intrinsic", "built-in", "ingrained"], "near_synonyms": [ \{ "word": "innate", "difference": "innate is strictly limited to living beings, meaning a trait born with a person or animal (like innate musical talent); inherent applies broadly to objects, systems, contracts, and abstract risks." \}, \{ "word": "intrinsic", "difference": "intrinsic emphasizes the absolute philosophical value or nature of a thing independent of external features or opinions." \} ], "antonyms": ["acquired", "extrinsic", "incidental"], "word_family": [ \{ "word": "inherent", "pos": "adjective" \}, \{ "word": "inhere", "pos": "verb" \}, \{ "word": "inherently", "pos": "adverb" \} ], "usage_note": "Inherent is frequently followed by the preposition 'in' when connecting a specific risk or trait to a task (e.g., risks inherent in skydiving).", "frequency_rank": 30, "frequency_source": "custom", "difficulty": "C1", "tags": ["college", "academic", "philosophy"] \}, \{ "word": "innovation", "pos": "noun", "phonetic": "/\u716 \u618 n\u601 \u712 ve\u618 \u643 n/", "definition": "The action or process of innovating; a new method, idea, or product brought forward.", "definition_zh": "\u21109 \u26032 \u65307 \u38761 \u26032 \u65307 \u26032 \u35264 \u24565 \u65307 \u26032 \u30332 \u26126 ", "etymology": \{ "roots": [ \{ "root": "in", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "into, thoroughly", "meaning_zh": "\u36914 \u20837 \u12289 \u24505 \u24213 " \}, \{ "root": "nov / novus", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "new", "meaning_zh": "\u26032 \u30340 " \} ] \}, "mnemonic": \{ "root_memory": "IN\u65288 \u36914 \u20837 \u65289 + NOV\u65288 \u26032 \u65289 \u8594  \u27880 \u20837 \u20840 \u26032 \u20803 \u32032 \u65292 \u25171 \u30772 \u33290 \u26377 \u39636 \u21046 ", "story_en": "The startup injected IN (into) the market a completely NOV (new) engine model, driving technological INNOVATION.", "story_zh": "\u36889 \u23478 \u26032 \u21109 \u20844 \u21496 \u23559 \u20840 \u26032 \u65288 NOV\u65289 \u30340 \u24341 \u25806 \u27169 \u24335 \u27880 \u20837 \u65288 IN\u65289 \u24066 \u22580 \u65292 \u25512 \u21205 \u20102 \u25216 \u34899 \u12300 \u21109 \u26032 \u65288 INNOVATION\u65289 \u12301 \u12290 ", "emojis": ["\u55357 \u56481 ", "\u55357 \u56960 ", "\u55357 \u56561 "] \}, "example": "Continuous technological innovation is crucial for companies trying to survive in competitive markets.", "example_zh": "\u23565 \u26044 \u35430 \u22294 \u22312 \u31478 \u29229 \u28608 \u28872 \u30340 \u24066 \u22580 \u20013 \u29983 \u23384 \u30340 \u20844 \u21496 \u20358 \u35498 \u65292 \u25345 \u32396 \u30340 \u25216 \u34899 \u21109 \u26032 \u33267 \u38364 \u37325 \u35201 \u12290 ", "collocations": [ "technological innovation", "foster innovation", "product innovation", "drive innovation", "constant innovation" ], "synonyms": ["invention", "novelty", "breakthrough", "revolution"], "near_synonyms": [ \{ "word": "invention", "difference": "invention means creating a brand-new scientific device or process that never existed before; innovation means applying or improving an existing concept commercially in a clever new way." \}, \{ "word": "novelty", "difference": "novelty refers to something that is new and unusual merely for amusement or entertainment value, without long-term social utility." \} ], "antonyms": ["stagnation", "tradition", "orthodoxy"], "word_family": [ \{ "word": "innovation", "pos": "noun" \}, \{ "word": "innovate", "pos": "verb" \}, \{ "word": "innovative", "pos": "adjective" \}, \{ "word": "innovator", "pos": "noun" \} ], "usage_note": "The verb form 'innovate' is intransitive; you innovate 
