\f0\i0  a field or sector, or use it as a standalone action.", "frequency_rank": 31, "frequency_source": "custom", "difficulty": "B2", "tags": ["college", "business", "technology"] \}, \{ "word": "integrate", "pos": "verb", "phonetic": "/\uc0\u712 \u618 nt\u618 \u609 re\u618 t/", "definition": "To combine one thing with another so that they become a whole, smooth, functioning system.", "definition_zh": "\u25972 \u21512 \u65307 \u20351 \u21512 \u20341 \u65307 \u34701 \u20837 \u32676 \u39636 ", "etymology": \{ "roots": [ \{ "root": "integr / integer", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "whole, untouched, complete", "meaning_zh": "\u23436 \u25972 \u30340 \u12289 \u26410 \u21463 \u35320 \u30896 \u30340 " \} ] \}, "mnemonic": \{ "root_memory": "INTEGR\u65288 \u23436 \u25972 \u65289 \u8594  \u25226 \u38646 \u30862 \u30340 \u37096 \u20998 \u25340 \u25104 \u19968 \u20491 \u23436 \u25972 \u30340 \u22291 \u28415 \u20491 \u39636 ", "story_en": "To make the system INTEGR (whole), engineers must INTEGRATE the separate database nodes smoothly.", "story_zh": "\u28858 \u20102 \u20351 \u31995 \u32113 \u12300 \u23436 \u25972 \u65288 INTEGR\u65289 \u12301 \u65292 \u24037 \u31243 \u24107 \u24517 \u38920 \u23559 \u29544 \u31435 \u30340 \u36039 \u26009 \u24235 \u31680 \u40670 \u38918 \u26274 \u22320 \u12300 \u25972 \u21512 \u65288 INTEGRATE\u65289 \u12301 \u36215 \u20358 \u12290 ", "emojis": ["\u55358 \u56809 ", "\u55357 \u56580 ", "\u55358 \u56605 "] \}, "example": "Immigrants often face significant cultural barriers when trying to integrate into a new society.", "example_zh": "\u31227 \u27665 \u22312 \u35430 \u22294 \u34701 \u20837 \u26032 \u31038 \u26371 \u26178 \u65292 \u24448 \u24448 \u38754 \u33256 \u24040 \u22823 \u30340 \u25991 \u21270 \u38556 \u31001 \u12290 ", "collocations": [ "integrate into", "fully integrate", "seamlessly integrate", "integrate technology", "fail to integrate" ], "synonyms": ["incorporate", "merge", "blend", "amalgamate"], "near_synonyms": [ \{ "word": "incorporate", "difference": "incorporate implies putting a minor item into a large pre-existing system; integrate implies a balanced blending where all elements adapt to create perfect harmony." \}, \{ "word": "merge", "difference": "merge usually describes two corporate entities of roughly equal size combining into a single new identity." \} ], "antonyms": ["segregate", "separate", "isolate"], "word_family": [ \{ "word": "integrate", "pos": "verb" \}, \{ "word": "integration", "pos": "noun" \}, \{ "word": "integrity", "pos": "noun" \}, \{ "word": "integrated", "pos": "adjective" \} ], "usage_note": "When talking about social groups or immigrants, 'integrate into' is the standard phrasing used by sociologists.", "frequency_rank": 32, "frequency_source": "custom", "difficulty": "B2", "tags": ["college", "academic", "social"] \}, \{ "word": "interpret", "pos": "verb", "phonetic": "/\u618 n\u712 t\u604 \u720 rpr\u601 t/", "definition": "To explain the meaning of information, words, or actions; or to translate orally between distinct languages.", "definition_zh": "\u35438 \u37323 \u65307 \u35299 \u37323 \u65307 \u35299 \u35712 \u65307 \u21475 \u35695 ", "etymology": \{ "roots": [ \{ "root": "inter", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "between, among", "meaning_zh": "\u22312 \'85\u20043 \u38291 " \}, \{ "root": "pret / pres", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "trader, negotiator, price broker", "meaning_zh": "\u20132 \u26131 \u32773 \u12289 \u20013 \u38291 \u21830 \u12289 \u35498 \u26126 \u20729 \u20540 \u32773 " \} ] \}, "mnemonic": \{ "root_memory": "INTER\u65288 \u20043 \u38291 \u65289 + PRET\u65288 \u20013 \u38291 \u21830 \u65289 \u8594  \u31449 \u22312 \u20841 \u32773 \u20043 \u38291 \u65292 \u24171 \u38617 \u26041 \u35299 \u37323 \u35338 \u24687 \u30340 \u30495 \u27491 \u21547 \u24847 ", "story_en": "Acting INTER (between) two kings, the PRET (negotiator) had to INTERPRET the foreign message accurately.", "story_zh": "\u31449 \u22312 \u20841 \u20301 \u22283 \u29579 \u12300 \u20043 \u38291 \u65288 INTER\u65289 \u12301 \u65292 \u36889 \u20301 \u35527 \u21028 \u20013 \u38291 \u21830 \u65288 PRET\u65289 \u24517 \u38920 \u31934 \u30906 \u22320 \u12300 \u35438 \u37323 /\u21475 \u35695 \u65288 INTERPRET\u65289 \u12301 \u36889 \u23553 \u22806 \u22283 \u20449 \u20214 \u12290 ", "emojis": ["\u55357 \u56803 \u65039 ", "\u55357 \u56522 ", "\u55358 \u56784 "] \}, "example": "Data analysts are required to interpret complex data charts and present actionable summaries to executives.", "example_zh": "\u25976 \u25818 \u20998 \u26512 \u24107 \u34987 \u35201 \u27714 \u35299 \u35712 \u35079 \u38620 \u30340 \u25976 \u25818 \u22294 \u34920 \u65292 \u20006 \u21521 \u39640 \u38542 \u20027 \u31649 \u25552 \u20132 \u20855 \u39636 \u21487 \u34892 \u30340 \u25688 \u35201 \u12290 ", "collocations": [ "interpret data", "difficult to interpret", "interpret laws", "differently interpret", "interpret dream" ], "synonyms": ["explain", "decode", "clarify", "construe"], "near_synonyms": [ \{ "word": "explain", "difference": "explain means to make something clear by providing facts or reasons; interpret implies that the information is deep, artsy, or cryptic, requiring subjective judgment to extract meaning." \}, \{ "word": "translate", "difference": "translate refers to converting written text from one language to another; interpret often implies converting spoken words live (oral translation) or adding intellectual commentary." \} ], "antonyms": ["misinterpret", "misunderstand", "confuse"], "word_family": [ \{ "word": "interpret", "pos": "verb" \}, \{ "word": "interpretation", "pos": "noun" \}, \{ "word": "interpreter", "pos": "noun" \} ], "usage_note": "A common academic mistake is using 'translate data' when they actually mean 'interpret data' (which means analyzing chart patterns).", "frequency_rank": 33, "frequency_source": "custom", "difficulty": "B2", "tags": ["college", "academic", "language"] \}, \{ "word": "justify", "pos": "verb", "phonetic": "/\u712 d\u658 \u652 st\