\f0\i0  smooth and logical on the surface." \}, \{ "word": "probable", "difference": "probable means something is mathematically likely to happen based on high statistics; plausible simply means it is realistic enough not to be dismissed." \} ], "antonyms": ["implausible", "unbelievable", "absurd"], "word_family": [ \{ "word": "plausible", "pos": "adjective" \}, \{ "word": "plausibility", "pos": "noun" \}, \{ "word": "plausibly", "pos": "adverb" \} ], "usage_note": "Watch out for political rhetoric, where an argument can be 'superficially plausible' but collapse under serious analytical scrutiny.", "frequency_rank": 44, "frequency_source": "custom", "difficulty": "C1", "tags": ["college", "academic", "law"] \}, \{ "word": "pragmatic", "pos": "adjective", "phonetic": "/pr\'e6\uc0\u609 \u712 m\'e6t\u618 k/", "definition": "Dealing with things sensibly and realistically in a way that is based on practical rather than theoretical considerations.", "definition_zh": "\u21209 \u23526 \u30340 \u65307 \u37325 \u23526 \u25928 \u30340 \u65307 \u23526 \u29992 \u20027 \u32681 \u30340 ", "etymology": \{ "roots": [ \{ "root": "pragma", "origin": "Greek", "meaning": "deed, act, concrete matter", "meaning_zh": "\u34892 \u28858 \u12289 \u20570 \u36942 \u30340 \u20107 \u12289 \u20855 \u39636 \u29289 " \} ] \}, "mnemonic": \{ "root_memory": "PRAGMA\u65288 \u20855 \u39636 \u34892 \u21205 \u65289 \u8594  \u19981 \u31354 \u35527 \u29702 \u35542 \u65292 \u30452 \u25509 \u30475 \u20855 \u39636 \u34892 \u21205 \u33287 \u23526 \u38555 \u32080 \u26524 ", "story_en": "Instead of reading pure philosophy, the CEO focused on the PRAGMA (deed) to take a PRAGMATIC step.", "story_zh": "\u33287 \u20854 \u38321 \u35712 \u32020 \u31929 \u30340 \u21746 \u23416 \u29702 \u35542 \u65292 \u22519 \u34892 \u38263 \u26356 \u23560 \u27880 \u26044 \u12300 \u20855 \u39636 \u34892 \u21205 \u65288 PRAGMA\u65289 \u12301 \u65292 \u25505 \u21462 \u12300 \u21209 \u23526 \u30340 \u65288 PRAGMATIC\u65289 \u12301 \u27493 \u39519 \u12290 ", "emojis": ["\u55357 \u57056 \u65039 ", "\u55357 \u56508 ", "\u55356 \u57263 "] \}, "example": "The two countries set aside historical grievances to forge a pragmatic economic partnership.", "example_zh": "\u20841 \u22283 \u25291 \u38283 \u20102 \u27511 \u21490 \u24681 \u24616 \u65292 \u24314 \u31435 \u36215 \u21209 \u23526 \u30340 \u32147 \u28639 \u22821 \u20276 \u38364 \u20418 \u12290 ", "collocations": [ "pragmatic approach", "pragmatic solution", "highly pragmatic", "pragmatic reason", "take a pragmatic view" ], "synonyms": ["practical", "realistic", "utilitarian", "hardheaded"], "near_synonyms": [ \{ "word": "practical", "difference": "practical is an everyday word for things or skills that work well (like practical shoes or advice); pragmatic is an intellectual or policy-level term, emphasizing choosing results over rigid ideological dogmas." \}, \{ "word": "utilitarian", "difference": "utilitarian strongly focuses on extreme efficiency and pure usefulness, often ignoring beauty, emotions, or artistic design entirely." \} ], "antonyms": ["idealistic", "theoretical", "visionary"], "word_family": [ \{ "word": "pragmatic", "pos": "adjective" \}, \{ "word": "pragmatism", "pos": "noun" \}, \{ "word": "pragmatist", "pos": "noun" \}, \{ "word": "pragmatically", "pos": "adverb" \} ], "usage_note": "A 'pragmatic approach' (\u21209 \u23526 \u30340 \u26041 \u27861 ) is one of the most high-frequency phrases used in business management and diplomacy.", "frequency_rank": 45, "frequency_source": "custom", "difficulty": "C1", "tags": ["college", "business", "philosophy"] \}, \{ "word": "preclude", "pos": "verb", "phonetic": "/pr\u618 \u712 klu\u720 d/", "definition": "To prevent from happening; to make something impossible, especially by taking action in advance.", "definition_zh": "\u25490 \u38500 \u65307 \u38450 \u27490 \u65307 \u20351 \u19981 \u21487 \u33021 \u65307 \u26460 \u32085 ", "etymology": \{ "roots": [ \{ "root": "pre", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "before", "meaning_zh": "\u22312 \'85\u20043 \u21069 " \}, \{ "root": "clud / claudere", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "to shut, close", "meaning_zh": "\u38364 \u38281 \u12289 \u38364 \u19978 " \} ] \}, "mnemonic": \{ "root_memory": "PRE\u65288 \u25552 \u21069 \u65289 + CLUD\u65288 \u38364 \u38272 \u65289 \u8594  \u25552 \u26089 \u25226 \u22823 \u38272 \u38364 \u19978 \u65292 \u23559 \u21361 \u38570 \u33287 \u21487 \u33021 \u24615 \u37782 \u22312 \u22806 \u38754 ", "story_en": "The guard will PRE (before) clude danger by slamming the gate CLUD (shut) to PRECLUDE illegal entry.", "story_zh": "\u35686 \u34907 \u34249 \u30001 \u12300 \u25552 \u21069 \u65288 PRE\u65289 \u12301 \u12300 \u38364 \u19978 \u65288 CLUD\u65289 \u12301 \u22823 \u38272 \u20358 \u38459 \u25803 \u21361 \u38570 \u65292 \u20197 \u12300 \u26460 \u32085 \u65288 PRECLUDE\u65289 \u12301 \u38750 \u27861 \u36914 \u20837 \u12290 ", "emojis": ["\u55357 \u56594 ", "\u55357 \u57003 ", "\u55357 \u57041 "] \}, "example": "A pre-existing medical condition does not automatically preclude you from buying travel insurance.", "example_zh": "\u26082 \u26377 \u30340 \u37291 \u30274 \u29376 \u27841 \u20006 \u19981 \u24517 \u28982 \u25490 \u38500 \u20320 \u36092 \u36023 \u26053 \u36938 \u20445 \u38570 \u30340 \u36039 \u26684 \u12290 ", "collocations": [ "preclude the possibility", "automatically preclude", "preclude from doing", "designed to preclude", "not preclude" ], "synonyms": ["prevent", "obviate", "forestall", "exclude"], "near_synonyms": [ \{ "word": "prevent", "difference": "prevent is a broad term meaning to stop an action or person from completing a task; preclude means to establish a logical rule or condition that makes an event completely impossible from the start." \}, \{ "word": "forestall", "difference": "forestall means to act quickly in advance to delay or ruin an opponent's planned strategy." \} ], "antonyms": ["permit", "allow", "facilitate"], "word_family": [ \{ "word": "preclude", "pos": "verb" \}, \{ "word": "preclusion", "pos": "noun" \} ], "usage_note": "Preclude is frequently paired with the fixed phrase 'preclude the possibility of...' (\u25490 \u38500 \'85\u30340 \u21487 \u33021 \u24615 ).", "frequency_rank": 46, "frequency_source": "custom", "difficulty": "C1", "tags": ["college", "academic", "law"] \}, \{ "word": "qualitative", "pos": "adjective", "phonetic": "/\u712 kw\u593 \u720 l\u618 te\u618 t\u618 v/", "definition": "Relating to, measuring, or measured by the quality of something (its traits and features) rather than its raw numeric quantity.", "definition_zh": "\u24615 \u36074 \u30340 \u65307 \u36074 \u21270 \u30340 \u65307 \u36074 \u24615 \u30340 ", "etymology": \{ "roots": [ \{ "root": "qual / qualis", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "of what kind, property", "meaning_zh": "\u20309 \u31278 \u39006 \u22411 \u12289 \u23660 \u24615 " \} ] \}, "mnemonic": \{ "root_memory": "QUAL\u65288 \u31278 \u39006 /\u21697 \u36074 \u65289 \u8594  \u35264 \u23519 \u20107 \u29289 \u30340 \u20839 \u22312 \u23660 \u24615 \u12289 \u29305 \u24501 \u33287 \u26412 \u36074 \u31278 \u39006 ", "story_en": "Researchers check the basic QUAL (kind) of behavior during a QUALITATIVE interview rather than counting digits.", "story_zh": "\u30740 \u31350 \u20154 \u21729 \u22312 \u36914 \u34892 \u12300 \u36074 \u24615 \u65288 QUALITATIVE\u65289 \u12301 \u35370 \u35527 \u26178 \u65292 \u26371 \u27298 \u26597 \u34892 \u28858 \u30340 \u20839 \u22312 \u12300 \u23660 \u24615 \u65288 QUAL\u65289 \u12301 \u32780 \u19981 \u26159 \u35336 \u31639 \u25976 \u23383 \u12290 ", "emojis": ["\u55357 \u56541 ", "\u55357 \u56522 ", "\u55358 \u56784 "] \}, "example": "The sociology department prefers qualitative research methods, such as open-ended focus groups, over statistical surveys.", "example_zh": "\u33287 \u32113 \u35336 \u35519 \u26597 \u30456 \u27604 \u65292 \u31038 \u26371 \u23416 \u31995 \u26356 \u20559 \u22909 \u36074 \u24615 \u30740 \u31350 \u26041 \u27861 \u65292 \u20363 \u22914 \u38283 \u25918 \u24335 \u30340 \u28966 \u40670 \u23567 \u32068 \u35370 \u35527 \u12290 ", "collocations": [ "qualitative research", "qualitative analysis", "qualitative data", "purely qualitative", "qualitative differences" ], "synonyms": ["descriptive", "subjective", "trait-based"], "near_synonyms": [ \{ "word": "descriptive", "difference": "descriptive simply means providing a detailed verbal picture of an event without requiring an academic methodology." \}, \{ "word": "quantitative", "difference": "quantitative is the direct antonym, meaning strictly focused on counting numbers, percentages, and statistical charts." \} ], "antonyms": ["quantitative", "numerical"], "word_family": [ \{ "word": "qualitative", "pos": "adjective" \}, \{ "word": "quality", "pos": "noun" \}, \{ "word": "qualitatively", "pos": "adverb" \} ], "usage_note": "'Qualitative analysis' (\u36074 \u24615 \u20998 \u26512 ) is widely used alongside 'quantitative analysis' in scientific research methodologies.", "frequency_rank": 47, "frequency_source": "custom", "difficulty": "C1", "tags": ["college", "academic", "social"] \}, \{ "word": "redundant", "pos": "adjective", "phonetic": "/r\u618 \u712 d\u652 nd\u601 nt/", "definition": "Not or no longer needed or useful; superfluous, or repeating data unnecessarily; (UK/corporate) laid off from a job because the position is no longer required.", "definition_zh": "\u22810 \u39192 \u30340 \u65307 \u36101 \u36848 \u30340 \u65307 \u34987 \u35009 \u21729 \u30340 ", "etymology": \{ "roots": [ \{ "root": "re", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "again, back", "meaning_zh": "\u20877 \u27425 \u12289 \u21521 \u24460 " \}, \{ "root": "und / unda", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "wave", "meaning_zh": "\u27874 \u28010 \u12289 \u27700 \u27969 " \} ] \}, "mnemonic": \{ "root_memory": "RE\u65288 \u20877 \u27425 \u65289 + UND\u65288 \u27874 \u28010 \u65289 \u8594  \u27700 \u28415 \u20102 \u21448 \u20877 \u27425 \u25472 \u36215 \u27874 \u28010 \u65292 \u28415 \u28322 \u20986 \u20358 \u35722 \u25104 \u22810 \u39192 \u30340 ", "story_en": "When water flows RE (again) as an extra UND (wave), the glass overflows and becomes REDUNDANT.", "story_zh": "\u30070 \u27700 \u27969 \u12300 \u20877 \u27425 \u65288 RE\u65289 \u12301 \u24418 \u25104 \u22810 \u39192 \u30340 \u12300 \u27874 \u28010 \u65288 UND\u65289 \u12301 \u26178 \u65292 \u26479 \u23376 \u23601 \u28322 \u20986 \u20358 \u20102 \u65292 \u35722 \u24471 \u12300 \u22810 \u39192 \u30340 \u65288 REDUNDANT\u65289 \u12301 \u12290 ", "emojis": ["\u55356 \u57098 ", "\u55357 \u56785 \u65039 ", "\u55357 \u56508 "] \}, "example": "Writing 'free gift' is grammatically redundant because all gifts are inherently free.", "example_zh": "\u23531 \u19979 \u12300 \u20813 \u36027 \u31150 \u29289 \u12301 \u22312 \u25991 \u27861 \u19978 \u26159 \u22810 \u39192 \u30340 \u65288 \u36101 \u36848 \u65289 \u65292 \u22240 \u28858 \u25152 \u26377 \u31150 \u29289 \u26412 \u36074 \u19978 \u37117 \u26159 \u20813 \u36027 \u30340 \u12290 ", "collocations": [ "make redundant", "redundant information", "become redundant", "highly redundant", "redundant features" ], "synonyms": ["superfluous", "unnecessary", "repetitive", "surplus"], "near_synonyms": [ \{ "word": "superfluous", "difference": "superfluous is a highly formal term for luxury items or extra assets that exceed what is useful or required; redundant implies unnecessary repetition within a communication system or workforce." \}, \{ "word": "repetitive", "difference": "repetitive means doing or saying the exact same thing over and over, which causes boredom but might not be useless." \} ], "antonyms": ["essential", "necessary", "concise"], "word_family": [ \{ "word": "redundant", "pos": "adjective" \}, \{ "word": "redundancy", "pos": "noun" \} ], "usage_note": "In British business English, 'to be made redundant' is the standard formal phrase for losing one's job due to structural downsizing.", "frequency_rank": 48, "frequency_source": "custom", "difficulty": "B2", "tags": ["college", "language", "business"] \}, \{ "word": "reinforce", "pos": "verb", "phonetic": "/\u716 ri\u720 \u618 n\u712 f\u596 \u720 rs/", "definition": "To strengthen or support an existing structure, feeling, habit, or military force with additional material or personnel.", "definition_zh": "\u21152 \u24375 \u65307 \u22686 \u25588 \u65307 \u24375 \u21270 \u65288 \u35264 \u24565 \u25110 \u32080 \u27083 \u65289 ", "etymology": \{ "roots": [ \{ "root": "re", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "again", "meaning_zh": "\u20877 \u27425 " \}, \{ "root": "in", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "into", "meaning_zh": "\u25237 \u20837 \u12289 \u36914 \u20837 " \}, \{ "root": "forc / fortis", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "strong", "meaning_zh": "\u24375 \u22767 \u12289 \u21147 \u37327 " \} ] \}, "mnemonic": \{ "root_memory": "RE\u65288 \u20877 \u27425 \u65289 + IN\u65288 \u20837 \u65289 + FORC\u65288 \u21147 \u37327 \u65289 \u8594  \u20877 \u27425 \u23559 \u21147 \u37327 \u25237 \u20837 \u20854 \u20013 \u20197 \u20351 \u32080 \u27083 \u26356 \u31337 \u22266 ", "story_en": "The general sent units RE (again) IN (into) the field to bring extra FORC (strength) to REINFORCE the broken line.", "story_zh": "\u23559 \u36557 \u12300 \u20877 \u27425 \u65288 RE\u65289 \u12301 \u23559 \u37096 \u38538 \u27966 \u12300 \u20837 \u65288 IN\u65289 \u12301 \u25136 \u22580 \u65292 \u24118 \u20358 \u38989 \u22806 \u30340 \u12300 \u21147 \u37327 \u65288 FORC\u65289 \u12301 \u20197 \u12300 \u22686 \u25588 /\u21152 \u24375 \u65288 REINFORCE\u65289 \u12301 \u26039 \u35010 \u30340 \u38450 \u32218 \u12290 ", "emojis": ["\u55356 \u57303 \u65039 ", "\u55358 \u56800 ", "\u55357 \u56490 "] \}, "example": "The teacher used positive rewards, like gold stars, to reinforce good behavior in the classroom.", "example_zh": "\u32769 \u24107 \u21033 \u29992 \u37329 \u26143 \u31561 \u27491 \u21521 \u29518 \u21237 \u20358 \u24375 \u21270 \u35506 \u22530 \u19978 \u30340 \u33391 \u22909 \u34892 \u28858 \u12290 ", "collocations": [ "reinforce concrete", "reinforce stereotype", "strongly reinforce", "reinforce habits", "reinforce the idea" ], "synonyms": ["strengthen", "bolster", "buttress", "fortify"], "near_synonyms": [ \{ "word": "strengthen", "difference": "strengthen is a general word meaning to make anything physically or mentally stronger; reinforce emphasizes adding extra physical layers, pillars, or evidence to an existing system." \}, \{ "word": "fortify", "difference": "fortify is heavily tied to adding military defensive structures (like walls or castles) or adding nutrients to food (fortified milk)." \} ], "antonyms": ["weaken", "undermine", "shake"], "word_family": [ \{ "word": "reinforce", "pos": "verb" \}, \{ "word": "reinforcement", "pos": "noun" \} ], "usage_note": "In social studies, 'reinforce stereotypes' (\u21152 \u28145 \u21051 \u26495 \u21360 \u35937 ) is a very frequent negative collocation.", "frequency_rank": 49, "frequency_source": "custom", "difficulty": "B2", "tags": ["college", "psychology", "academic"] \}, \{ "word": "relevant", "pos": "adjective", "phonetic": "/\u712 rel\u601 v\u601 nt/", "definition": "Closely connected or appropriate to what is being done, discussed, or considered at that moment.", "definition_zh": "\u30456 \u38364 \u30340 \u65307 \u20999 \u38988 \u30340 \u65307 \u26377 \u37325 \u22823 \u38364 \u20418 \u30340 ", "etymology": \{ "roots": [ \{ "root": "re", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "again, back", "meaning_zh": "\u20877 \u27425 \u12289 \u21521 \u24460 " \}, \{ "root": "lev / levare", "origin": "Latin", "meaning": "to lift, raise", "meaning_zh": "\u25260 \u36215 \u12289 \u33289 \u36215 " \} ] \}, "mnemonic": \{ "root_memory": "RE\u65288 \u20877 \u27425 \u65289 + LEV\u65288 \u25260 \u36215 \u65289 \u8594  \u20877 \u27425 \u25226 \u20999 \u38988 \u12289 \u26377 \u29992 \u30340 \u37325 \u35201 \u35264 \u40670 \u33289 \u36215 \u20358 \u20379 \u22823 \u23478 \u35342 \u35542 ", "story_en": "The speaker chose to RE (again) LEV (lift) the primary data point because it was highly RELEVANT to the debate.", "story_zh": "\u28436 \u35611 \u32773 \u36984 \u25799 \u12300 \u20877 \u27425 \u65288 RE\u65289 \u12301 \u12300 \u25260 \u39640 \u33289 \u36215 \u65288 LEV\u65289 \u12301 \u26680 \u24515 \u25976 \u25818 \u65292 \u22240 \u28858 \u36889 \u33287 \u36783 \u35542 \u20027 \u38988 \u12300 \u39640 \u24230 \u30456 \u38364 \u65288 RELEVANT\u65289 \u12301 \u12290 ", "emojis": ["\u55357 \u56599 ", "\u55356 \u57263 ", "\u55357 \u56541 "] \}, "example": "Please include all relevant work experience on your resume when applying for the senior managerial role.", "example_zh": "\u30003 \u35531 \u39640 \u38542 \u31649 \u29702 \u32887 \u20301 \u26178 \u65292 \u35531 \u22312 \u23653 \u27511 \u20013 \u21253 \u21547 \u25152 \u26377 \u30456 \u38364 \u30340 \u24037 \u20316 \u32147 \u39511 \u12290 ", "collocations": [ "highly relevant", "relevant information", "directly relevant", "relevant experience", "remain relevant" ], "synonyms": ["applicable", "germane", "pertinent", "apposite"], "near_synonyms": [ \{ "word": "applicable", "difference": "applicable means a rule or system 
\f1\i can
\f0\i0  legally or technically be used in a specific scenario; relevant emphasizes that the information is helpful and logically connected to the ongoing conversation." \}, \{ "word": "pertinent", "difference": "pertinent is a formal, precise term meaning perfectly and directly hitting the exact core of a specific issue without any extra fluff." \} ], "antonyms": ["irrelevant", "unrelated", "inappropriate"], "word_family": [ \{ "word": "relevant", "pos": "adjective" \}, \{ "word": "relevance", "pos": "noun" \}, \{ "word": "relevantly", "pos": "adverb" \} ], "usage_note": "Relevant is standardly followed by the preposition 'to' (e.g., facts relevant to the legal trial).", "frequency_rank": 50, "frequency_source": "custom", "difficulty": "B1", "tags": ["college", "academic", "business"] \} ]}